---
title: "Status"
description: "What is built, what is genuinely not done, and what is cut rather than pending."
---

Accurate as of 2026-07-31. Three things gate real use, and they are listed first.

## The three gates

::: {.callout-important}
## 1. No smoke run has been done

`docs/SMOKE.md` is the manual checklist and the **only** acceptance basis for
`registration.ts`, `checkin.ts`, `reports.ts`, `group.ts`, `reminders.ts` and the
Telegram calls inside `ticker.ts`.

Not one phase's checklist has been worked through. Everything below "is built" in
the sense that the code exists, typechecks and passes 264 automated tests. None of
it has been observed working against the real Bot API.
:::

::: {.callout-important}
## 2. Nothing is deployed, so there is no off-machine backup

NFR-3 is met **by the deployment target**, not by code in this repository. The
intended home is Tietokilta's shared Azure PostgreSQL server, whose databases are
dumped and shipped off-site nightly by infrastructure outside this repo.

That move has not happened. The infra change has not been raised. Until it does,
a Compose volume is the only copy of anything, and it is not one anybody should
rely on.
:::

::: {.callout-important}
## 3. The competition dates are placeholders

`COMPETITION_START` and `COMPETITION_END` in `src/config.ts` are plausible-shaped
stand-ins. `SPEC.md` §9 Q1 is unresolved.

Guild `memberCount` values are also **unverified**, and they are the denominator of
every ranking.
:::

## What is built

| Phase | Contents | State |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Registration, `/log`, `/me`, `/standings`, the check-in flow, undo, backdating | Code complete |
| 2 | Group chat binding, the pinned standings, the Monday post | Code complete |
| 3 | Daily reminders, the five-ignore auto-stop and follow-up, `/remind`, 403 handling | Code complete |
| 4 | The restore-fidelity test and the decoder hardening | Code complete, **phase not complete** |

Phase 4 is the one worth being precise about. Its code is done: `tests/db/restore.test.ts`
proves a dump restores into an empty database and reproduces every published
number exactly in both dump formats, and `decode()`'s re-encode assertion closes
trailing-junk payloads. But the phase **reduces to NFR-3**, and NFR-3 is met by
deploying this bot's database onto Tietokilta's infrastructure, which has not
happened.

## Cut, not pending

Do not implement these. Each was decided against with a reason recorded.

| Item | Where the decision lives |
|---|---|
| FR-11's optional tag on a logged day | `SPEC.md` §9 Q5. The `days.tag` column exists and nothing writes it |
| A Telegram Mini App | `SPEC.md` §8 |
| MET-based scoring / a sport taxonomy | `SPEC.md` §8 |
| A global individual leaderboard | `SPEC.md` §8 and FR-15 |
| Per-user timezones | `SPEC.md` §8 |

`SPEC.md` §8 is the canonical list. **Read it before adding anything that looks
obviously missing**, because most of what looks obviously missing is there on
purpose.

## Open questions

`SPEC.md` §9 tracks these in three groups: still open, decided, and unresolved but
not blocking. The two that matter operationally are Q1 (the competition dates) and
the unverified member counts, both covered above.

## Budget

`src/` is **1,958 effective lines** against NFR-6's 2,000-line ceiling, so **42
remain**.

The history is worth knowing, because it explains why there is so little room:

- Phase 4 spent 4 of the 84 that remained after Phase 3, because its work was tests
  and documents rather than application logic, and because FR-11 was cut.
- The pre-smoke bugfix pass spent 38 more, on the FR-9 undo guard and the FR-12 week
  label.

There is no longer room for a feature here, only for fixes. The next change that
needs more than 42 lines is a conversation about the ceiling rather than a quiet
overrun.

`SPEC.md` §7's per-phase line split was a rounded residual rather than a costed
estimate, and a note there records this so the conflict is not rediscovered.

## What a maintainer should do first

In order:

1. Resolve the competition dates and set them (`SPEC.md` §9 Q1).
2. Verify every guild's member count with the guild itself.
3. Work through `docs/SMOKE.md` end to end against a test bot.
4. Raise the infra change so the database exists on Tietokilta's server, which is
   what makes NFR-3 true.

Steps 1 to 3 are inside this repository. Step 4 is not, and it is the one that has
been outstanding longest.
